= Integrated circuit — Simple =

<onlyinclude>An '''integrated circuit''' (IC), often called a '''chip''', is an electronic circuit in which many components and their connections are formed together on or within a piece of material, usually a semiconductor such as silicon. A chip may contain transistors, resistive and capacitive structures, wires and other devices. Integration makes complex electronic functions much smaller and can improve production consistency, but a chip's performance depends on design, manufacturing, power delivery and packaging.<ref name="smithsonian">Smithsonian Institution, [https://www.si.edu/object/integrated-circuit-jack-kilby%3Anmah_689592 ''Integrated Circuit by Jack Kilby''], a 1958 demonstration and historical object record.</ref></onlyinclude>

== What is inside a chip? ==

Some integrated circuits perform simple tasks such as amplifying a signal or managing power. Others contain large digital systems, including processors and memory.

A digital chip uses electronic switching circuits, including [[Logic gate/Simple|logic gates]], to perform computations. A different chip might contain analog circuits, high-frequency components or light-sensitive structures.

The material underneath the components is called a '''die'''. Many dies are built on a large wafer and later separated. A packaged IC can include electrical contacts and protective material around its die. One package may also contain multiple dies.

== How is a chip made? ==

Factories build structures on wafers using repeated steps such as applying thin films, exposing patterns with light, removing selected material, introducing dopants, forming insulating layers and adding metal interconnections.

After fabrication, devices are tested; suitable dies may be cut from the wafer, packaged and tested again. A working circuit design does not guarantee every manufactured die will work, because production varies and defects can occur.

== Two important historical milestones ==

Jack Kilby demonstrated an early integrated circuit at Texas Instruments in 1958. Robert Noyce developed a practical monolithic circuit approach in 1959, building on Jean Hoerni's planar process and connections formed over an insulating layer. These were distinct contributions; the invention and production history is not one person's single step.<ref name="history">Computer History Museum, [https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/practical-monolithic-integrated-circuit-concept-patented/ ''1959: Practical Monolithic Integrated Circuit Concept Patented''] and [https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/first-planar-integrated-circuit-is-fabricated/ ''1960: First Planar Integrated Circuit Is Fabricated''].</ref>

== Why does integration matter? ==

Placing devices together can reduce the distance signals must travel, lower some manufacturing costs at high volume, and make circuits repeatable. But larger, denser chips introduce heat, power, communication and fabrication challenges.

A chip may have millions or billions of transistors, but transistor count alone does not tell you how fast, efficient or reliable it is. Architecture, clocking, software, memory, manufacturing variation and the workload all matter.

== Design versus manufacturing ==

Engineers create circuit diagrams, hardware descriptions, layouts and verification tests. A factory uses manufacturing processes to turn the design into physical layers.

Some companies design chips and use a separate foundry to fabricate them. Other companies combine design and manufacturing. The work also involves packaging, testing, design software and suppliers of materials and equipment.

== How do engineers evaluate a chip? ==

A useful evaluation considers what the chip is designed to do, its power and voltage requirements, how quickly it works, its accuracy, cost, thermal limits and reliability.

For a new circuit design, engineers verify that the digital logic does what was intended and check that physical timing, manufacturing rules and safety margins are satisfied.

== What is not an integrated circuit? ==

A printed circuit board (PCB) connects separately packaged components using traces on a board. It can contain many ICs but is not itself usually called a single monolithic integrated circuit. Likewise, a computer processor is one kind of IC, not the definition of every IC.

